{"text": "Smoking and Cardiovascular Disease\n\nP, Hill and E. Wynder\nAmerican Health Foundation, New York, N.Y. 10021\n\n. SUMMARY\n\nHabitual smokers after a 40 minute rest period were requested\nto smoke two cigarettes consecutively. Prior and after smoking,\nthe serum catecholamines, corticoids and free fatty acids (FFA)\ncontents were determined. Increasing the nicotine per cigarette\nprogressively increased the adrenaline in the blood, while no\nchange in noradrenaline occurred. Although the FFA content in the\nserum was increased, no significant relation to the nicotine content\nwas noted. Under these conditions, an increase in cortisol oceurred\nafter the highest exposure to nicotine or after more prolonged\nsmoking of cigarettes with a lower nicotine content.\n\nZpidemiological evidence has well demonstrated cigarette\nsmoking increases the incidence of myocardial infarction as well\nas sudden death(1,2). This effect occurs primarily in populations\nwith a high rate of atherosclerosis(3) and suggests that cigarette\nsmoking enhances the role of the underlying atherosclerosis. The\n\u2018observation that cigarette smoking increases the risk of cerebral\nthrombosis and peripheral vascular disease (PVD) as well as aortic\naneurysms also indicates that cigarette smoking has a deleterious\neffect on peripheral blood vessels(4).\nA reduction in the risk of myocardial infarction(MI) has been\nreported to occur rapidly(5) or decrease by about one third, one year\nafter cessation of smoking(6). \u2018These differences in the decrease in\nrisk of MI among ex-smokers remains to be explored further.\nCigar and pipe smokers have a lower risk of MI than cigarette\nsmokers (1). Gsell & Abelin(7) have shown that the risk of MI appears\nto be a function of the degree of inhalation of tobacco smoke. \u00a9\nDuring smoking, rapid absorption of carbon monoxide and nicotine\noccurs and over 90% of the nicotine inhaled may be absorbed(8,9). 8", "words": [{"text": "Smoking", "x0